# Study Content Quality

When turning uploaded notes/slides into condensed notes, flashcards, or quizzes for CORE:

## Prioritize substance
- Extract **testable concepts**: definitions, frameworks, formulas, tradeoffs, causal relationships, decision rules, worked examples, and how to apply them.
- Prefer material that would appear on an exam over anything that only orients the class session.

## Skip class logistics (do not turn into cards/quiz items)
- Syllabus, grading, due dates, office hours, attendance
- Agenda slides (“today we will…”, learning objectives lists with no content)
- Professor intros, guest speakers, housekeeping, LMS links
- Assignment instructions unless they encode a concept worth remembering
- Purely decorative titles, section dividers, or “questions?” slides

## Crafting guidance
- Flashcards: one clear concept per card; prompt should force recall of the idea, not the slide title.
- Quizzes: ask about meaning, application, comparison, and calculation — not “what was covered in week 3.”
- Condensed notes: keep key terms + must-know bullets; drop filler and meta commentary.
- If slides mix logistics and content, **strip the logistics** and only keep the academic payload.
